PLACES=[
N('idaho-city-historic-district','Idaho City (Bannock) gold-rush town','Idaho City','id','town','standing',43.82861,-115.83222,'area','public','walk','','1862–',
  'Founded in December 1862 as Bannock in the Boise Basin gold rush and renamed Idaho City by the territorial legislature. Wikipedia gives it 7,000 people in 1864, the largest city in the Northwest, with more than 200 businesses. Built of wood, it burned in 1865, 1867, 1868 and 1871; the Register form says the rebuilding after the fires was mostly in brick and stone. The whole town inside its 1975 limits is a National Register district, and it is still the Boise County seat.',
  'A frontier main street of board-and-batten and false-fronted wooden buildings among one-storey red brick blocks with round-arched openings (the Register form names the Miners Exchange block, the Idaho World building and the Boise Basin Mercantile). The separate entries here cover the church, the lodges, the museum, the old territorial jail, the courthouse and the pioneer cemetery. Pon Yam\'s 1867 store is one of the few survivals of the Chinese town (Wikipedia).',
  '',
  'A living town: walk the public streets and look from them. Most buildings are private or businesses. '+ICTOWN,
  [ICW,ICNR,HM(273124,'Idaho City')],brick='some'),
N('st-josephs-church-idaho-city','St. Joseph\'s Catholic Church, Idaho City','Idaho City','id','worship','standing',43.8269,-115.8322,'site','open','walk','','1867',
  'The parish was established in 1863 and its first church dedicated that November; it burned in the town fire of May 17, 1867, and the present church was put up within weeks (the HMdb marker gives June 2, 1867; the Register form says it was erected in 30 days). The parish calls it the oldest Catholic church built for Euro-Americans in Idaho.',
  'A plain white clapboard frame church high on Church Hill, reached from the street by a long flight of wooden steps, with a steep gable roof, an enclosed gabled entry, narrow windows of eight stained-glass panes each, and a short open belfry with a cross (Register form, 1974). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'An active chapel of Our Lady of the Rosary parish, Boise: Mass on the first and third Sundays at noon and the fourth and fifth Sundays at 10:30 am (parish page). Other opening not checked. '+ICTOWN,
  [['Our Lady of the Rosary parish: St. Joseph\'s, Idaho City','https://olrboise.org/st-josephs-chapel'],HM(119109,'Saint Joseph\'s Catholic Church'),ICNR],brick='no'),
N('boise-basin-museum-idaho-city','Boise Basin Museum (1867 post office)','Idaho City','id','civic','standing',43.82846,-115.83379,'exact','open','walk','','1867',
  'Built in 1867 by James A. Pinney as the post office and a book shop; it also served as a Wells Fargo office and, in the 1880s, as one of the homes of the Idaho World newspaper. A drug-store wing was added about 1868. The city leased it in 1957 to what is now the Idaho City Historical Foundation, which runs it as the Boise Basin Museum.',
  'A low one-storey brick building with a low-pitched gable roof, porches front and side, and parts of two old dentilled cornices in the front gable (Register form). Inside: the gold-rush collection, photographs from the 1860s on and archives (Clio). Mining and logging machinery stands in John Brogan Park beside it (HMdb).',
  '',
  'Run by the Idaho City Historical Foundation at 503 Montgomery Street. The foundation\'s own page is drawn by script and could not be read; Clio quotes Visit Idaho for summer hours of Monday to Saturday plus Sunday 11 am to 4 pm. Hours not checked with the foundation. '+ICTOWN,
  [HM(119062,'Boise Basin Museum'),['Clio: Boise Basin Museum','https://theclio.com/entry/12113'],ICNR],brick='?'),
N('idaho-territorial-penitentiary-idaho-city','Idaho Territorial Penitentiary building, Idaho City','Idaho City','id','civic','standing',43.828399,-115.835,'site','view','walk','','1864',
  'The territorial and county jail, built in May 1864 by G. B. Walker for $10,975 and used jointly by Idaho Territory and Boise County. It first stood on Buena Vista Bar west of town; it was moved in 1930 to make way for gold mining, moved twice more, and was set on its present lot in 1953 by the Sons and Daughters of Idaho Pioneers (HMdb marker).',
  'The old jail building beside the Masonic Temple at Wall and Commercial streets, with its marker. What it is built of and whether it is opened to visitors was not checked.',
  '',
  'Seen from Wall Street; who keeps it now and whether it opens was not checked. '+ICTOWN,
  [HM(119105,'Idaho Territorial Penitentiary'),ICW],brick='?'),
N('masonic-temple-idaho-city','Masonic Temple (Idaho Lodge No. 1), Idaho City','Idaho City','id','civic','standing',43.828433,-115.834313,'site','view','walk','','1865',
  'Built in September 1865 after the lodge\'s first hall burned in the fire of May 1865. The Grand Lodge of Idaho was formed here in December 1867. Idaho Lodge No. 1 moved to Boise in 1920 but still meets here each June; the Grand Lodge and the lodge keep it jointly (HMdb marker). It was one of the few buildings the later fires spared (Register form).',
  'A two-storey wooden hall with an enclosed outside stair to the upper floor, a walkway and an upper porch each on five wooden posts, three sets of 10-foot double doors with glazed upper halves, board-and-batten sides and a clapboard front (Register form). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A private lodge hall; seen from Wall Street. '+ICTOWN,
  [HM(119104,'Masonic Temple'),ICNR],brick='no'),
N('ioof-hall-idaho-city','I.O.O.F. Hall (Pioneer Lodge No. 1), Idaho City','Idaho City','id','civic','standing',43.827598,-115.831458,'site','view','walk','','1875',
  'Home of Pioneer Lodge No. 1, the oldest Odd Fellows lodge in Idaho, organized in 1864. The HMdb marker says the hall was completed in September 1875 for $1,974 and opened that November, and was heavily rebuilt in 1941; the 1974 Register form gives the building as 1855, which is before the lodge or the town existed, so the marker\'s date is used here.',
  'A two-storey wooden hall high on the hillside above the town, with an enclosed outside stair, a verandah on four posts with a balustraded porch above, and a semicircular plaque in the gable over painted I.O.O.F. initials. The form says the original carpeting and furniture were still in use in 1974. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A private lodge hall on East Walulla Street; seen from the street. '+ICTOWN,
  [HM(119107,'I.O.O.F. Hall'),ICNR],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('boise-county-courthouse-idaho-city','Boise County Courthouse, Idaho City','Idaho City','id','civic','standing',43.82817,-115.83334,'exact','view','walk','','1870s',
  'A brick building at Wall and Main streets that has been the county courthouse since 1909, when the county bought it from Mary Orchard for $1,000 (HMdb marker). The sources disagree on its start: the marker says C. S. and E. A. Kingsley built it as a general store from July to October 1871, and the Register form describes the courtroom building as built in 1870 as a hotel and restaurant. Frank Church announced his run for president from its porch in March 1976 (Wikipedia).',
  'A one-storey red brick building with a covered walkway all round on plain wooden posts, and tall double doors with paneled iron shutters (Register form). The marker says the inside was remodelled to look as it once did, with the furniture of the 1863 courthouse on Commercial Street. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Boise County\'s working courthouse; public hours not checked. '+ICTOWN,
  [HM(109935,'Boise County Courthouse'),ICNR,ICW],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('pioneer-cemetery-idaho-city','Idaho City Pioneer Cemetery (Boot Hill)','Idaho City','id','burial','ruins',43.83182,-115.84193,'site','view','walk','cemeteries','1860s–',
  'The gold-rush burying ground north-west of town. The IDGenWeb page quotes an estimate of about 3,000 burials, of which fewer than 300 graves can now be identified. Wikipedia names the Boot Hill cemetery among the town\'s historic sites.',
  'Graves under the pines; the number of old wooden markers and fenced plots left was not checked.',
  '',
  'Off Buena Vista Road; owner and hours not checked. The nearest mapped lot is on Buena Vista Road about 540 m south; parking was not checked on the ground.',
  [['IDGenWeb: Idaho City\'s Pioneer Cemetery','https://boise.idgenweb.org/cem_idacity.html'],ICW],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('placerville-historic-district-idaho','Placerville gold camp and its two museums','Placerville','id','town','standing',43.943,-115.9455,'site','public','drive','','1862–',
  'A Boise Basin camp laid out on December 1, 1862; by early summer 1863 it had 300 buildings and 5,000 people, and it had 48 people at the 2020 census. Its settled area became a Register district in 1984. On the town square the Boise Basin Mercantile, built in the 1860s as one of the first stores in Placerville, and the Magnolia Saloon, rebuilt in 1900 after the August 1899 fire, are both museums (HMdb markers).',
  'The Mercantile with period goods on its shelves and the lone cell of the town jail in its basement; the Henrietta Penrod Museum in the old Magnolia Saloon, with pictures and records of the town (HMdb).',
  '',
  'A small incorporated city on the square at Granite Street and Standifer Road. Wikipedia says the two museums are kept by volunteers and open at weekends from Memorial Day to Labor Day and on request; hours not checked with the Placerville Historical Society. Parking not checked.',
  [['Wikipedia: Placerville, Idaho','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Placerville,_Idaho'],HM(119031,'Boise Basin Mercantile Museum'),HM(119030,'Henrietta Penrod Museum')],brick='?'),
N('arrowrock-dam-idaho','Arrowrock Dam','Boise','id','water','standing',43.59559,-115.92259,'exact','view','drive','','1911–1915',
  'A concrete arch dam on the Boise River for irrigation, opened in 1915 by the Reclamation Service. At about 350 feet it was the highest dam in the world until 1934 (HMdb marker) and a prototype for later Western gravity-arch dams; a railroad was laid up the old Idaho City wagon road to build it. It became a National Historic Civil Engineering Landmark in 2016 and is still worked by the Bureau of Reclamation.',
  'The curved concrete face across the canyon; the civil engineers\' 2016 plaque is on Arrowrock Road (HMdb gives its position).',
  '',
  'Operated by the U.S. Bureau of Reclamation; seen from Arrowrock Road, off State Highway 21. Road conditions, closures and where to stop were not checked.',
  [['Wikipedia: Arrowrock Dam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arrowrock_Dam'],HM(263631,'Arrowrock Dam (ASCE)'),HM(22597,'Arrowrock Dam')],brick='no'),
N('old-idaho-penitentiary-boise','Old Idaho Penitentiary','Boise','id','inst','standing',43.6027,-116.162,'site','open','walk','','1870–1973',
  'Idaho Territory\'s prison, begun with a single cell house in 1870 and in use from 1872 to 1973. It grew to a group of buildings inside a 17-foot wall of sandstone that the convicts quarried from the ridges nearby; more than 13,000 prisoners passed through it, 216 of them women (Wikipedia). The Idaho State Historical Society runs it as a state historic site, one of four territorial prisons open to the public (its own page).',
  'The sandstone wall and cell houses, the 1893 addition, the women\'s ward, the sally port and the warden\'s house just outside (HMdb markers), with self-guided and guided tours inside the yard; some upstairs rooms are reached only by stairs.',
  '',
  'Idaho State Historical Society, 2445 Old Penitentiary Road. Open seven days a week except major state holidays: winter 12 to 5 pm, summer (Memorial Day to Labor Day) 10 am to 5 pm, last admission 4 pm. Admission $9 adults, $7 seniors, $5 ages 6 to 12; guided tours $2 more (ISHS page, read November 2026). Parking on Old Penitentiary Road; the lot was not checked on the aerial.',
  [['Idaho State Historical Society: Old Idaho Penitentiary','https://history.idaho.gov/oldpen/'],['Wikipedia: Old Idaho State Penitentiary','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Idaho_State_Penitentiary'],HM(63000,'Idaho State Penitentiary')],brick='?'),
N('assay-office-boise','U.S. Assay Office, Boise','Boise','id','civic','standing',43.61271,-116.19663,'exact','view','walk','','1870–1871',
  'The first major federal building in Idaho Territory: Congress gave $75,000 in 1869 for an office to assay the territory\'s gold, and it was built in 1870 and 1871 on a block given by Alexander Rossi. It closed as an assay office in 1933, served the Forest Service until 1972, and then passed to the Idaho State Historical Society. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1961.',
  'A two-storey building about 45 feet high of local Boise sandstone, walls more than two feet thick, coursed rustic stone with smooth quoins at the corners and windows, a low hip roof and a small central ventilator cupola (landmark nomination). Inside, the ground floor held the assay rooms, vaults and furnaces and the upper floor the chief assayer\'s home (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Idaho State Historical Society, at 210 Main Street; seen from the street. Whether it opens to visitors was not checked. Downtown street parking.',
  [['Wikipedia: Assay Office (Boise, Idaho)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assay_Office_(Boise,_Idaho)'],['Assay Office (National Historic Landmark nomination)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000305_text']],brick='no'),
N('ofarrell-cabin-boise','John A. O\'Farrell Cabin','Boise','id','landmark','standing',43.61887,-116.1944,'exact','view','walk','','1863',
  'A one-room cottonwood-log cabin that John O\'Farrell built in June 1863 for his wife and family; a 1915 Daughters of the American Revolution plaque calls it the first home in Boise to shelter women and children. In 1864 he gave it a sawn-rafter shingle roof, board-and-batten gables, plank floors and walls and a brick fireplace (HMdb markers).',
  'The squared cottonwood logs with steeple-notched corners and a five-row hand-split shingle roof, with several interpretive markers beside it on West Fort Street. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'On West Fort Street near Fourth Street, at the edge of the old Fort Boise grounds; seen from the sidewalk. Who opens it and when was not checked. Street parking.',
  [HM(267155,'The O\'Farrell Cabin'),HM(267011,'First Home in Boise'),NR('99001415','John A. O\'Farrell Cabin')],brick='?'),
N('boise-union-pacific-depot','Boise Depot (Union Pacific)','Boise','id','rail','standing',43.602,-116.2147,'site','view','walk','stations','1925',
  'Union Pacific\'s mainline station, opened in April 1925 on the bench above the city with a 96-foot bell tower that looks straight down Capitol Boulevard to the Capitol. Morrison Knudsen bought it from the Union Pacific and had it restored, a $3.4 million job finished in 1993, and the City of Boise took it over in 1996 (Wikipedia, city pages). The Union Pacific laid out the gardens below it in 1927.',
  'Stucco walls, a clay-tile roof and the tall bell tower, lit at night; inside, the Great Hall with railroad memorabilia and a model of the depot. The city says the Great Hall and tower are closed for restoration until about late August 2027; the gardens (Platt Gardens) are a city park.',
  '',
  'City of Boise Parks and Recreation. Closed for restoration work, expected until late August 2027 (city page, read November 2026); when open it has been open Sundays and Mondays from 11 am. Parking not checked.',
  [['City of Boise: Boise Depot','https://www.cityofboise.org/departments/parks-and-recreation/facilities/boise-depot/'],['City of Boise: History of the Boise Depot','https://www.cityofboise.org/departments/parks-and-recreation/facilities/boise-depot/history-of-the-boise-depot/'],['Wikipedia: Boise Union Pacific Depot','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boise_Union_Pacific_Depot']],brick='no'),
N('swan-falls-dam-powerhouse','Swan Falls Dam and old powerhouse','Kuna','id','water','standing',43.24302,-116.37982,'site','public','drive','','1901',
  'The first hydroelectric dam on the Snake River, built in 1901 to send power to the Owyhee mines round Silver City. Idaho Power bought it in 1916 and in 1994 built a new powerhouse beside the old one, which is now a museum. The dam and plant are on the National Register, inside the Morley Nelson Snake River Birds of Prey National Conservation Area.',
  'The 1901 powerhouse with its museum displays, the old dam and spillway across the Snake, and the canyon walls round it.',
  '',
  'Idaho Power\'s free day-use park at the dam, about 20 miles down Swan Falls Road south of Kuna (BLM). The museum is open Saturdays 10 am from mid-April to Labor Day, and by arrangement a week ahead; it was closed for the season when read in November 2026. Walking across the dam is closed for spillway work (Idaho Power page). The nearest mapped lot is about 900 m south-east on Swan Falls Road.',
  [['Idaho Power: Swan Falls Park','https://idahopower.com/community-recreation/recreation/parks-and-campgrounds/swan-falls-park/'],['BLM: Swan Falls Dam','https://www.blm.gov/visit/swan-falls-picnic-area'],['Wikipedia: Swan Falls Dam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swan_Falls_Dam']],
  th=[43.23562,-116.37473],thn='Lot on South Swan Falls Road',brick='?'),
N('guffey-railroad-bridge','Guffey Railroad Bridge','Melba','id','rail','standing',43.29883,-116.5301,'exact','public','walk','','1897–1947',
  'A 500-foot, 450-ton steel Parker through-truss bridge, about 70 feet high, built in 1897 for the Boise, Nampa and Owyhee Railroad across the Snake River; the only bridge of its kind in Idaho. The sources differ on what it carried: Wikipedia says it was built to bring Silver City ore to the Nampa smelter, while the Canyon County marker and the Idaho Heritage Trust say the mines closed before it was finished and no ore went over it, though sheep and cattle did. Abandoned in 1947 and saved from scrap, it was bought and restored by Canyon County in 1989.',
  'The steel truss across the canyon, open to walkers and riders, with trails on the south bank; the petroglyphs of Celebration Park are just upstream.',
  '',
  'Canyon County\'s Celebration Park, off Sinker Road near Melba, with the BLM\'s land on the far bank. The bridge is restored for non-motorized use (BLM page). Park hours and the day-use fee were not checked: the county\'s park page would not open.',
  [['BLM: Celebration Park','https://www.blm.gov/visit/celebration-park'],HM(73344,'Guffey Bridge Snake River Idaho'),['Idaho Heritage Trust: Historic Guffey Bridge','https://www.idahoheritagetrust.org/?p=3981'],['Wikipedia: Celebration Park','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celebration_Park']],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('silver-city-historic-district-idaho','Silver City','Silver City','id','town','standing',43.01694,-116.73306,'area','view','drive','','1864–',
  'A silver and gold camp founded in 1864 below War Eagle Mountain; at its height in the 1880s it had about 2,500 people and some 75 businesses, and it was the Owyhee County seat until 1934. Idaho Territory\'s first daily newspaper and telegraph office were here. It declined after the mines gave out around 1890 but was never quite abandoned (Wikipedia).',
  'About 70 standing buildings, all privately owned; the Idaho Hotel, restored and reopened for visitors in 1972, is one of three businesses (Wikipedia, the town\'s site).',
  '',
  'The buildings are private: look from the streets. Reached by a dirt road 25 miles from near Murphy, off State Highway 78 (Wikipedia; BLM campground page). Winter road conditions were not checked; the Idaho Hotel was closed for the season when its site was read in November 2026. The BLM keeps a small campground in town; do not drink from Jordan Creek, which may carry mercury from old mining (BLM).',
  [['Wikipedia: Silver City, Idaho','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_City,_Idaho'],['Historic Silver City, Idaho','https://historicsilvercityidaho.com/'],['BLM: Silver City Campground','https://www.blm.gov/visit/silver-city-campground'],NR('72000446','Silver City historic district')],brick='?'),
N('our-lady-queen-of-heaven-oreana','Our Lady, Queen of Heaven Church, Oreana','Oreana','id','worship','standing',43.040287,-116.394637,'site','view','drive','','c. 1883',
  'A small lava-rock building of about 1883 in the ranching hamlet of Oreana, built with a false front over its gable; it became a Catholic church in a 1961 renovation, which added the belfry, cross, buttresses and new porch (Register form, 1980).',
  'Coursed lava-rubble walls with deep-set shuttered windows under relieving arches of dressed lava rock and a lava-rock chimney, all of the 1880s; the belfry, cross, gable buttresses and porch are of 1961. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Seen from the road in Oreana; owner and service times not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [NR('80001333','Our Lady, Queen of Heaven Church')],brick='no'),
N('wickahoney-stage-station','Wickahoney Post Office and Stage Station','Wickahoney','id','landmark','ruins',42.45609,-115.98387,'site','view','hike','','1895–1911',
  'A lava-rock ranch house that Dow Dunning built on his homestead, which served as Wickahoney\'s post office from 1895 to 1911 and as a stop on the stage road from Mountain Home, Idaho to Mountain City, Nevada. When the stage traffic moved to a route through Elko the place faded away (Wikipedia). The Register form (1982) calls it one of only two lava-rock stage stations left in Idaho.',
  'The walls of a one-and-a-half-storey lava-rock house that once had seven gables, with a lava-rock barn and a stone corral behind. It has since lost its roof and porch (Wikipedia).',
  '',
  'In remote desert in southern Owyhee County, reached by dirt roads; land ownership was not checked (the 1982 form called it private) and nothing here says the public may go up to it: treat it as look-only. Take water, fuel and a spare. Pin is the Register coordinate.',
  [['Wikipedia: Wickahoney Post Office and Stage Station','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wickahoney_Post_Office_and_Stage_Station'],NR('82002514','Wickahoney Post Office and Stage Station')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
]

